<!-- SECTION 1: Dataset Overview -->
<!-- =========================== -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</h2>

  <p class="text-block">
    The dataset contains <strong>20,058 completed chess games</strong> collected from Lichess.org. Of these,
    <strong>16,155 games (80.5%)</strong> were rated, meaning player Elo ratings were affected by the outcome, while
    <strong>3,903 games (19.5%)</strong> were casual (unrated). Every game includes full metadata: player identities
    and ratings, time controls, opening classification (ECO code and name), complete move sequences, and detailed
    outcome information.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>Game Outcomes by Result</h4>
      <canvas id="chartOutcomeDonut"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>Victory Status Distribution</h4>
      <canvas id="chartVictoryBar"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="text-block">
    Games predominantly end via <strong>resignation (55.6%)</strong>, reflecting the convention in online chess where
    players concede lost positions rather than playing to checkmate. <strong>Checkmate accounts for 31.5%</strong> of
    games, while <strong>timeout (8.4%)</strong> and <strong>draws (4.5%)</strong> round out the distribution. The
    low draw rate (4.5%) is typical of amateur online play, where decisive results are far more common than in elite
    over-the-board competition.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key observation:</strong> White won 10,001 games (49.9%), Black won 9,107 (45.4%), with 950 draws (4.7%).
    Excluding draws, white wins <strong>52.34%</strong> of decisive games &mdash; a 4.7 percentage-point edge. This
    quantifies the well-known "first-move advantage" in chess and serves as the baseline for deeper analysis in the
    following sections.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- =========================== -->
<!-- SECTION 2: Color Advantage -->
<!-- =========================== -->
<section id="color-advantage">
  <h2>2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>

  <p class="text-block">
    Across all 20,058 games, white holds a clear but modest advantage. Of the 19,108 decisive games,
    white won <strong>52.34%</strong> to black's 47.66%. This +4.68 percentage-point edge translates to
    a <strong>white-to-black win ratio of 1.098</strong>: for every 100 games black wins, white wins approximately 110.
  </p>

  <h3>White Advantage by Rating Bracket</h3>
  <p class="text-block">
    The first-move advantage is not uniform across skill levels. Breaking games into rating brackets
    (using the white player's Elo) reveals a striking pattern: <strong>white's edge grows substantially
    as player strength increases</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <h4>White Win Rate by Rating Bracket (Excluding Draws)</h4>
    <canvas id="chartColorByRating"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table class="stats-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rating Bracket</th><th>Games</th><th>White Wins</th><th>Black Wins</th><th>Draws</th><th>White Win % (excl. draws)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below 1100</td><td>679</td><td>196</td><td>442</td><td>41</td><td class="negative">30.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1100 &ndash; 1400</td><td>4,368</td><td>1,880</td><td>2,325</td><td>163</td><td class="negative">44.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1400 &ndash; 1600</td><td>5,789</td><td>2,883</td><td>2,655</td><td>251</td><td class="positive">52.1%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1600 &ndash; 1800</td><td>4,342</td><td>2,305</td><td>1,856</td><td>181</td><td class="positive">55.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1800 &ndash; 2000</td><td>3,073</td><td>1,628</td><td>1,259</td><td>186</td><td class="positive">56.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000+</td><td>1,807</td><td>1,109</td><td>570</td><td>128</td><td class="positive">66.1%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout green">
    <strong>The gradient is steep:</strong> At the beginner level (&lt;1100), black actually wins <strong>69.3%</strong> of decisive games &mdash; suggesting that at very low ratings, random blunders overwhelm any positional advantage. By the 2000+ expert tier, white's edge expands dramatically to <strong>66.1%</strong>, nearly doubling the baseline advantage.
  </div>

  <h3>White Advantage by Time Control</h3>
  <p class="text-block">
    Time pressure affects how players convert the first-move advantage. Across the most popular time controls,
    white's edge is remarkably stable at the blitz and rapid levels:
  </p>

