<section id="rating-analysis">
  <h2>3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>

  <p class="text-block">
    Player ratings on Lichess span a wide range: <strong>white ratings from 784 to 2,700</strong> (mean: 1,596.6)
    and <strong>black ratings from 789 to 2,723</strong> (mean: 1,588.8). The distribution is centered around
    the intermediate range (1,400&ndash;1,600), where the bulk of online play occurs.
  </p>

  <h3>Rating Differential vs. Win Probability</h3>
  <p class="text-block">
    The rating differential &mdash; the difference between the white and black player's Elo &mdash; is the single
    strongest predictor of game outcome. Below, we bin games by the white-minus-black rating gap and plot
    white's win probability in each bin.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-box">
    <h4>White Win Probability by Rating Differential (White Rating &minus; Black Rating)</h4>
    <canvas id="chartRatingDiff"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table class="stats-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rating Differential</th><th>Sample Size</th><th>White Win Rate</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>&minus;500+ (black much stronger)</td><td>589</td><td class="negative">14.2%</td><td>Black dominant; white wins ~1 in 7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&minus;400 to &minus;500</td><td>398</td><td class="negative">18.3%</td><td></td></tr>
      <tr><td>&minus;200 to &minus;400</td><td>2,048</td><td class="negative">24.2%</td><td>Significant black edge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&minus;100 to &minus;200</td><td>2,195</td><td class="negative">36.1%</td><td>Moderate black edge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&minus;50 to &minus;100</td><td>1,803</td><td class="negative">43.9%</td><td>Slight black edge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&minus;50 to 0</td><td>2,638</td><td>50.0%</td><td>Near-even (white's move compensates slight rating deficit)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0 to +50</td><td>2,787</td><td class="positive">53.0%</td><td>Slight white edge (first-move + small rating edge)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+50 to +100</td><td>1,906</td><td class="positive">60.6%</td><td>Moderate white edge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+100 to +200</td><td>2,396</td><td class="positive">66.8%</td><td>Strong white edge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+200 to +400</td><td>2,149</td><td class="positive">78.8%</td><td>White strongly favored</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+400+ (white much stronger)</td><td>1,149</td><td class="positive">88.1%</td><td>White dominant; black wins ~1 in 8</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Notable asymmetry:</strong> When white is rated 50&ndash;100 points lower than black (diff &minus;50 to &minus;100),
    white still wins 43.9% of the time. Conversely, when white is rated 50&ndash;100 points higher, white's win rate
    jumps to 60.6%. This asymmetry &mdash; roughly <strong>16 percentage points</strong> is partially attributable to
    the first-move advantage: it offsets a ~50-point rating deficit but amplifies a ~50-point rating surplus.
  </div>

  <h3>Game Length by Rating Tier</h3>
  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>Average Turns by Rating Bracket</h4>
      <canvas id="chartTurnsByRating"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-box">
      <h4>Victory Status Distribution by Rating Bracket</h4>
      <canvas id="chartVictoryByRating"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="text-block">
    <strong>Game length increases with player strength.</strong> Beginners (below 1100) average 48.8 turns per game,
    while 1800&ndash;2000 players average 65.8 turns &mdash; a 35% increase. This reflects stronger players' ability to
    defend difficult positions and convert advantages methodically rather than via early blunders.
  </p>

  <p class="text-block">
    <strong>Resignation becomes the dominant end as ratings rise.</strong> At the 2000+ level, 66.7% of games end
    in resignation versus only 47.7% at the beginner level. Meanwhile, checkmate rates drop from 40.5% (below 1100)
    to just 17.7% (2000+). Stronger players recognize lost positions earlier and resign rather than playing to mate,
    a marker of chess maturity.
  </p>
</section>

