    in bullet to 18.7% in classical), <strong>resignation rates rise</strong> (from 41.1% to 69.6%), and
    <strong>draw rates increase</strong> (from 3.7% to 8.9%). Players with more time are more willing to play
    out positions, leading to more accurate assessments and timely resignations. Classical games also run longer
    (72.9 turns average) and show the highest white win rate (53.6%), consistent with the idea that the
    first-move advantage is best exploited when players have time to think.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- =========================== -->
<!-- SECTION 6: Key Findings -->
<!-- =========================== -->
<section id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>

  <div class="key-finding">
    <div class="num">1</div>
    <div>
      <strong>White's advantage is real but modest.</strong> Across all 20,058 games, white wins 52.34% of decisive
      games &mdash; a 4.68 pp edge. This confirms the first-move advantage, but it is far from decisive: in nearly
      half of all games, black overcomes it. The effect is strongest at high ratings (66.1% white wins at 2000+)
      and weakest at low ratings (30.7% below 1100, where random blunders dominate).
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="key-finding">
    <div class="num">2</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Rating differential is the dominant predictor.</strong> A 100&ndash;200 point rating advantage
      pushes win probability to ~67% for the higher-rated player; a 400+ point gap pushes it to 88%.
      The relationship is non-linear: small rating gaps (&plusmn;50 points) produce near-even outcomes,
      but the win probability curve steepens rapidly beyond a 100-point differential.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="key-finding">
    <div class="num">3</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Not all openings are created equal for white.</strong> The Philidor Defense (C41, white win 59.7%),
      Owen Defense (B00, 62.6%), and McConnell Defense (C40, 61.4%) are white's best-performing popular openings.
      Conversely, the Van't Kruijs Opening (A00, 41.1%) and Bowdler Attack (B20, 41.1%) see black winning more often
      than white. Opening depth correlates with white success: 6+ ply openings see 52.9% white wins vs. 45.7% for
      0&ndash;2 ply openings.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="key-finding">
    <div class="num">4</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Stronger players resign; weaker players get checkmated.</strong> At the 2000+ level, 66.7% of games
      end in resignation and only 17.7% in checkmate. Below 1100, the pattern inverts: 47.7% resignation, 40.5%
      checkmate. This shift is a clear marker of chess maturity &mdash; recognizing lost positions and conserving
      mental energy for the next game.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="key-finding">
    <div class="num">5</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Time controls shape the game's character.</strong> Bullet games are chaotic: 14% end by timeout,
      and an equal share end by checkmate and resignation. Classical games are methodical: 69.6% resignation,
      only 2.8% timeout. White's advantage grows with slower controls (51.1% bullet &rarr; 53.6% classical),
      and draws also increase from 3.7% to 8.9%.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="key-finding">
    <div class="num">6</div>
    <div>
      <strong>Game length increases with skill.</strong> Beginners average 48.8 turns per game; experts (1800&ndash;2000)
      average 65.8 turns &mdash; 35% longer. Drawn games are the longest at 83.8 turns, and resigned games the
      shortest at 53.9 turns. This suggests that stronger players' longer games reflect both deeper middlegame
      knowledge and more resilient defense, not simply slower play.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="key-finding">
    <div class="num">7</div>
    <div>
      <strong>The first-move advantage compounds with rating.</strong> A player 50 points stronger with white
      wins 60.6% of the time; a player 50 points stronger with black wins only 56.1% (white's win rate flipped).
